#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715831 :question/problem "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $r$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{r}{|r|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| &= \\sqrt{(6)^2+(8)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $r$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{r}{|r|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (r) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715833 :question/problem "Let $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|b| &= \\sqrt{(-8)^2+(-6)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715839 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{a}{|a|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a| &= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{a}{|a|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715845 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{a}{|a|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a| &= \\sqrt{(-8)^2+(6)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{a}{|a|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715859 :question/problem "Let $r=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $r$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{r}{|r|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| &= \\sqrt{(-4)^2+(-3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $r$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{r}{|r|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (r) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716098 :question/problem "![The parabola from (-1,0) through its vertex (-2,1) to (2,3), with traversal arrows.](images/00/00384c711760782c3d4b5d7801ee1292116fd53679d365affb98e921365fac32.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $(y-1)^2=x+2$ from $(-1,0)$ to $(2,3)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate." :question/worked-solution "The equation is simplest as a function of $y$:\n\n$$\nx=(y-1)^2-2.\n$$\n\nSet $y=t$. This follows the whole arc, including its vertex, with one parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle (t-1)^2-2,t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter bounds are the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints, not their $x$-coordinates. Differentiate to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2(t-1),1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{4(t-1)^2+1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into $f(x,y)=xy$ gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_Cxy\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^3 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^3 t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big)\\sqrt{4(t-1)^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $2(t-1)$ changes sign at the vertex, but its square in the speed keeps $\\mathrm{d}s$ nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716105 :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C (3x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^2\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x-2y=1$ from $(1,0)$ to $(5,2)$. Which expression is missing under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t$.*" :question/worked-solution "Solve the curve equation for $x$ and set $y=t$:\n\n$$\nx=1+2y,\\qquad \\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1+2t,t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThe bounds are the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints. The arc-length factor is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle 2,1\\rangle,\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=3(1+2t)-t=3+5t.\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(3x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^2 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^2(3+5t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $3+5t$; the factor $\\sqrt{5}$ is already outside the integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716112 :question/problem "![The reciprocal curve from (3,1) to (1/2,6), with arrows showing traversal.](images/ee/ee8e5ae8e5ae5ea03a9c4c8208db9b44ba978621d318719bdc546f0b62493bc4.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C 2xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of $x=\\dfrac{3}{y}$ from $(3,1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac12,6\\right)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t$.*" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ in $x=3/y$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{t},t\\right\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 6.\n$$\n\nUse the $y$-coordinates, $1$ and $6$, for the bounds. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\left\\langle-\\frac{3}{t^2},1\\right\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^4}+1}\n=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge1$, the denominator $t^2$ is positive. Substituting into the scalar function gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)t^2=6t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C2xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_1^6 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_1^6 6t\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=6\\int_1^6\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative $x$-component of the velocity is squared in the arc-length factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716121 :question/problem "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C\\frac{6x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x=y^2$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt6)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$, so $x=t^2$. The parameter bounds come from the endpoints' $y$-coordinates:\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le\\sqrt6.\n$$\nCompute the derivative and its magnitude:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle2t,1\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{4t^2+1}.\n$$\nSince $t\\ge1$, the denominator is nonzero and\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=\\frac{6t^2}{t}=6t.\n$$\nThus the scalar line integral is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\frac{6x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_1^{\\sqrt6}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=6\\int_1^{\\sqrt6}t\\sqrt{4t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=4t^2+1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=8t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. When $t=1$, $u=5$; when $t=\\sqrt6$, $u=25$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6\\int_1^{\\sqrt6}t\\sqrt{4t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n&=\\frac34\\int_5^{25}u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\frac34\\left[\\frac23u^{3/2}\\right]_5^{25}\\\\\n&=\\frac12\\left(25^{3/2}-5^{3/2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{125-5\\sqrt5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716128 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 2y\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The $y$-coordinates of the endpoints give $2\\le t\\le3$, so\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2t-4,t\\rangle,\\qquad 2\\le t\\le3.\n$$\nDifferentiate and compute the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle2,1\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\\sqrt5.\n$$\nThe scalar function becomes $f(\\mathbf r(t))=2t$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C 2y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_2^3 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\int_2^3 2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\left[t^2\\right]_2^3\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5(9-4)\\\\\n&=5\\sqrt5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716137 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 3x^6e^{-2y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=e^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt3,\\ln3)$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. Substituting into the curve gives\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle e^{t/2},t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le\\ln3.\n$$\nThe parameter bounds are the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints. The derivative requires a chain-rule factor of $\\tfrac12$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac12e^{t/2},1\\right\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\frac14e^t+1}\n=\\frac12\\sqrt{e^t+4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function and combine the exponentials:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=3(e^{t/2})^6e^{-2t}\\\\\n&=3e^{3t}e^{-2t}\\\\\n&=3e^t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C3x^6e^{-2y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^{\\ln3}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\frac32\\int_0^{\\ln3}e^t\\sqrt{e^t+4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=e^t+4$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=e^t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The bounds become $u=5$ and $u=7$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac32\\int_0^{\\ln3}e^t\\sqrt{e^t+4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n&=\\frac32\\int_5^7u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\frac32\\left[\\frac23u^{3/2}\\right]_5^7\\\\\n&=7^{3/2}-5^{3/2}\\\\\n&=7\\sqrt7-5\\sqrt5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716434 :question/problem "![](images/da/da44dc52332fab5cee40203fd6d0979cfa86bdc6d166c96372ba4e77b12476d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The intercepts are $(\\pm4,0)$ and $(0,\\pm3)$, so the horizontal radius is $4$ and the vertical radius is $3$. Each radius is the distance from the center to the ellipse, not the full axis length.\n\nAn ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=4$ and $b=3$, so\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe cosine and sine each range from $-1$ to $1$, giving exactly the horizontal and vertical extents shown."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716437 :question/problem "![](images/76/767fd967e905084a50f0a5d4ab1e2c9ad3e8e9bf62777a608100c418d8863a2a.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $6$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $A(4,0)$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=4$ and $b=6$, so\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, these equations give $(4,0)$. As $\\theta$ increases from $0$ to $\\pi/2$, the point moves from $(4,0)$ to $(0,6)$ along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse. This is counterclockwise. The remaining quarter-turns pass through $(-4,0)$ and $(0,-6)$; the interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal.\n\nThe pair $x=4\\sin\\theta,\\ y=6\\cos\\theta$ traces the same ellipse, but starts at $(0,6)$ and moves clockwise. Matching only the shape is insufficient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716438 :question/problem "![](images/12/12cbe2b704e91e62b6405f46868b226a1fbcf56e17da9b0927c798639c7795f5.png)\n\nThe ellipse shown is centered at the origin. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse it once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "Read the radii from the graph: the horizontal intercepts are $(\\pm3,0)$ and the vertical intercepts are $(0,\\pm2)$. Thus the horizontal radius is $3$ and the vertical radius is $2$.\n\nAn ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=3$ and $b=2$, so\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe marked point is $A(3,0)$. At $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, so the equations start at $A$. For increasing $\\theta$, the point travels through\n\n$$\n(3,0)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (0,2)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (-3,0)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (0,-2)\n$$\n\nat parameter values $0$, $\\pi/2$, $\\pi$, and $3\\pi/2$, respectively. This order is counterclockwise. As $\\theta$ approaches $2\\pi$, the point returns toward $A$, completing one traversal without including the starting point a second time."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716459 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+2,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-1,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$\n\nEnter only the left-hand side of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Undo each translation before dividing by the trigonometric coefficient. From the $x$-equation,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\theta+2\\\\\nx-2&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{x-2}{3}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $y$-equation,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\theta-1\\\\\ny+1&=4\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{y+1}{4}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}=1,\n$$\n\nso enter $\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}.$ The center is $(2,-1)$ and the horizontal and vertical radii are $3$ and $4.$ The full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716468 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe trigonometric terms have been isolated for you:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}=\\cos\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{y}{2}=\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSquare both equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$ Choose the resulting Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Square each isolated equation, including its denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{x^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta,\\\\\n\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{y^2}{4}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these equations and apply the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe parameter interval covers a full turn, so this equation describes the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716475 :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate $\\theta$ using the Pythagorean identity. First isolate the trigonometric terms and square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{x}{3}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{x^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{y}{4}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{y^2}{16}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nThe full interval $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$ traces the whole ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716477 :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes, with dashed horizontal and vertical lines through its center.](images/fb/fbf33b01d4b8dd517d4ce08d5d65bfd66560a208efe27fa88999cb267c0a433c.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations, defined for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$, generates the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes on the graph are $x=-2$ and $x=6$, and the vertical extremes are $y=-4$ and $y=2$. Their midpoints give the center:\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{-2+6}{2}=2,\\qquad k=\\frac{-4+2}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal and vertical radii are half the corresponding full widths:\n\n$$\na=\\frac{6-(-2)}{2}=4,\\qquad b=\\frac{2-(-4)}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSubstituting gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta-1,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThese equations reproduce all four extreme coordinates on the graph. The other choices swap the radii, reverse the translations, or use diameters or squared radii instead of radii."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716479 :question/problem "An ellipse has center $(-1,2)$, horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $2$.\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates this ellipse for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=-1$, $k=2$, $a=3$ and $b=2$. Use the radii as the coefficients, and add each coordinate of the center to the corresponding expression:\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta-1,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta+2.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal range is $[-4,2]$ and the vertical range is $[0,4]$, so these equations have the required center and radii. The other choices swap the radii, reverse the translations, use diameters, or use squared radii."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716484 :question/problem "![Ellipse with dashed center lines, its rightmost point A, and a counterclockwise arrow.](images/c6/c633c884fac6ead15c6ac06934ceb82280ae783aba26c136dc63f8099ee17055.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-1)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Construct parametric equations, defined for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$, that traverse the ellipse once counterclockwise, starting at $A(1,-1)$ when $\\theta=0$.\n\nUse sine or cosine of $\\theta$, without changing its argument.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe center is $(-2,-1)$ and the radii are $a=3$, $b=2$, so\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta-2,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta-1.\n$$\n\nWe must also check the starting point and direction. At $\\theta=0$, these give $(x,y)=(1,-1)=A$. At successive quarter turns they give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0 &: (1,-1),\\\\\n\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2} &: (-2,1),\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi &: (-5,-1),\\\\\n\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2} &: (-2,-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the motion is from the rightmost point to the top, then left and bottom: counterclockwise. Scaling and translating one full turn of $(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)$ traces the ellipse once; excluding $2\\pi$ avoids repeating the starting point.\n\nAs a check on the shape,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{x+2}{3}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{y+1}{2}\\right)^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716599 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f8b73b2bed7ec7f13267d8aea0c297292fdbaa5e7f4caeab12526cff50f0f3f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $4$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $H(3,0)$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=3$ and $b=4$, so\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $H(3,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (3,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,4)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-3,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-4).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716604 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cef92b657c3af8e56c23b8c82687dcc90bcea76a161b066bc224b8fbd9f95af.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $8$ and vertical radius $3$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $A(8,0)$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=8$ and $b=3$, so\n\n$$\nx=8\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $A(8,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (8,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,3)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-8,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716608 :question/problem "![](images/16/16655f767e57ba984b436683a914f943c9a557cd6c29250c0a365a09aec6b7f1.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $6$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $A(1,0)$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=1$ and $b=6$, so\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $A(1,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (1,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,6)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-1,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-6).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716615 :question/problem "![](images/ac/acfd1b409c19ca2d27d84106568f3e543da599bea88e144f008adfccdd31716d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $H(3,0)$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=3$ and $b=5$, so\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=5\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $H(3,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (3,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,5)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-3,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-5).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716619 :question/problem "![](images/50/50d0e14dc1d07ee58e2ede72997e6215c68b0d9d0b20492092e5e44ea85571c2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $J(4,0)$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=4$ and $b=2$, so\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $J(4,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (4,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,2)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-4,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716657 :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/52/52c0369db51ccca8d99135a8c4814952780eba670829a2f66eb2d8be0c7cfede.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 5 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{3}=\\ln y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(6,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}=\\ln y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=5 y e^{x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5 y e^{x} \\\\ &=5\\cdot t^{1}e^{3\\ln t} \\\\ &=5\\cdot t^{1}\\left(t^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=5 t^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 5 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(5 t^{4})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}5 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716659 :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/52/52c0369db51ccca8d99135a8c4814952780eba670829a2f66eb2d8be0c7cfede.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2} + 1}{t^2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{|t|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 y e^{x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(e^{1\\ln t}\\right)t^{1} \\\\ &=2\\left(t^{1}\\right)t^{1} \\\\ &=2 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(2 t^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716660 :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/68/68d39defe7e4584c6b645163402cfbaefca793311f9849f8ee249bed57dc086e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{3}{y}$ traversed between the points $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$ and $(\\frac{3}{4},4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{3}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\frac{3}{t},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 2\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^4}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{4} + 9}{t^4}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 9}}{t^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 x y^{3},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)t^{3} \\\\ &=6 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}(6 t^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}6 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716668 :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/07/07427c24e1419d2ea56169077343928792bd9116cb71cf46a9ad4a1662b914a6.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 3 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=- y^{2}$ traversed between the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(-9,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=- t^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle - t^{2},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad -2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $-2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 2 t)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 2 t)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left(- t^{2}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=3 t^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{3}(3 t^{5})\\cdot \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{3}3 t^{5} \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716682 :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/1c/1cc539b4c2ebce9ebcc17dc39e7c9dec840fe55cb5e4162aaad5719b400f33fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 3 x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{y}$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},6)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{4}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\frac{4}{t},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{4}{t^{2}}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{4}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{t^4}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{4} + 16}{t^4}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 16}}{t^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=3 x y^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=12 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{6}(12 t)\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{12 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 16}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716693 :question/problem "![](images/68/68d8212cddf5e8b7d6d3036582d0cbbae1b37b67e1725781a2a78e1bc8a1fd17.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ between $(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{2},2 \\sqrt{6})$ and $(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{2},\\sqrt{3})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $\\sqrt{3}\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{6}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=2 t^{3} e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $e^{-\\ln t}=1/t$ because $t>0$.\n\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $4$ and $25$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{4}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{4}^{25} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{250}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=78\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716696 :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6f4294d412a929e06bb9d858983e171d4e0d5c422fcf9d68f809f1abd0a2439.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ between $(0,1)$ and $(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{2},2 \\sqrt{6})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{6}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=3 t^{3} e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=3 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $e^{-\\ln t}=1/t$ because $t>0$.\n\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}3 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $2$ and $25$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{25}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{u}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[u^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right]_{2}^{25} \\\\ &=\\left(125\\right)-\\left(2 \\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ &=125 - 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716704 :question/problem "![](images/bf/bffe2a9f729c630c79dac8a71c31d163546767a13cf00a032d28171d19f6e2a3.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y - 5$ between $(1,3)$ and $(-3,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 3$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle 2 t - 5,t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2,1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+1}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{3}{- \\left(2 t - 5\\right) + t}=- \\frac{3}{t - 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- \\frac{3}{t - 5}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{t - 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=5 - t$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $4$ and $2$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{4}^{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[- 3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{4}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(- 3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBoth logarithm arguments are positive. The identity $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ combines the endpoint logarithms.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716714 :question/problem "![](images/55/5561c5106c9e0e95b7dbe6f7d0b646bae7236c36291332aa1f410bf2946e7d53.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ between $(1,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},3)$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 3$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle - \\frac{1}{t^{2}},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) t^{6}=t^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(t^{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}}{t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{4} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=4 t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $2$ and $82$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{82}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{6}\\right]_{2}^{82} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716719 :question/problem "![](images/21/21e7f28e62e188408bb3d5e5e535aed9ea735d0cdc9bb2b7c42ffc5d937b9837.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C \\frac{4 x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{2}$ between $(2,\\sqrt{2})$ and $(20,2 \\sqrt{5})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $\\sqrt{2}\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{5}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2 t,1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+1}=\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{4 \\left(t^{2}\\right)}{t}=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{4 x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2 \\sqrt{5}}\\left(4 t\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2 \\sqrt{5}}4 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=8 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $9$ and $81$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{4 x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{9}^{81}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{9}^{81} \\\\ &=\\left(243\\right)-\\left(9\\right) \\\\ &=234\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716722 :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/3b/3b7e6f3ad9d8198373fbc2f28198b80a4deeb02603a84c356792a1a2b14a230b.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $0$ and $4$; the vertical extremes are $-11$ and $-1$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{0+(4)}{2}=2, &k&=\\frac{-11+(-1)}{2}=-6,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{4-(0)}{2}=2, &b&=\\frac{-1-(-11)}{2}=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=2$, $k=-6$, $a=2$ and $b=5$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta-6,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716730 :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/7f/7f993f69b67f49ac59f934c67fa0d4117a3b554b4ba3652dc59e7da1ca8e3e2d.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-2$ and $6$; the vertical extremes are $-9$ and $7$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-2+(6)}{2}=2, &k&=\\frac{-9+(7)}{2}=-1,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{6-(-2)}{2}=4, &b&=\\frac{7-(-9)}{2}=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=2$, $k=-1$, $a=4$ and $b=8$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=8\\sin\\theta-1,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716731 :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/52/521c3413e9421fd4e7552bc4eb7d3af19ce13d7363f050f2772ccb4a296ba389.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-2$ and $2$; the vertical extremes are $-6$ and $2$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-2+(2)}{2}=0, &k&=\\frac{-6+(2)}{2}=-2,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{2-(-2)}{2}=2, &b&=\\frac{2-(-6)}{2}=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=0$, $k=-2$, $a=2$ and $b=4$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716744 :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/a7/a7d683f01ece53f00ba40f3c47fa8330dc02f30622552bf3a38976dbd9c0a5ff.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-2$ and $14$; the vertical extremes are $-1$ and $11$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-2+(14)}{2}=6, &k&=\\frac{-1+(11)}{2}=5,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{14-(-2)}{2}=8, &b&=\\frac{11-(-1)}{2}=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=6$, $k=5$, $a=8$ and $b=6$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=8\\cos\\theta+6,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta+5,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716750 :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/ee/ee41622e1810846b00fe003eb5dd264285fe31da94059a8293c21b60fb3e0cad.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-1$ and $5$; the vertical extremes are $-1$ and $1$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-1+(5)}{2}=2, &k&=\\frac{-1+(1)}{2}=0,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{5-(-1)}{2}=3, &b&=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=2$, $k=0$, $a=3$ and $b=1$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}]}
